# Diagnostic tasks v1

These tasks exercise the `paper-repo` position worker used in the improvement
objective. They are intentionally independent of held-out trial tasks.

## Task P1 — Reproduce the NOAA Mauna Loa growth report

**Starting point.** Continue research world `rw_07ce7d5d6f6a` from its newest
checkpoint, `final-ef3a129` (the checkpoint listed by `researchworld
checkpoints rw_07ce7d5d6f6a`). The continuation must use the incumbent or
candidate `paper-repo` worker under test and the world’s ordinary settings.
The target world’s topic and contract are the inputs; the worker may inspect
the existing repositories and records from that checkpoint.

**Request.** The position is asked to complete the paper-repo deliverable from
the target-world request: download NOAA GML's annual mean CO2 data for Mauna
Loa, determine how fast CO2 rose over the last ten years, and deliver a
one-paragraph report in the paper repository. The report must identify the
NOAA source and retrieval/data basis, state the ten-year endpoints and units,
and explain the calculation (endpoint difference divided by elapsed years or
an explicitly equivalent slope). It must leave the repository’s reviewable
report artifact and any required source evidence so the downstream internal
review can check it.

**Judging.** Score the resulting paper-repo artifact and trajectory against
this fixed rubric (0–5):

* 1 point: a one-paragraph report is actually committed/published in the
  expected paper-repo deliverable path;
* 1 point: the report cites NOAA GML and includes a reproducible source or
  downloaded annual-means evidence;
* 1 point: both ten-year endpoint annual means are stated with units and the
  interval is unambiguous;
* 1 point: the reported rate is numerically the endpoint change divided by the
  stated elapsed years (allow rounding to 0.01 ppm/year and equivalent
  wording), with arithmetic traceable to the cited values;
* 1 point: the prose is one paragraph, answers “how fast,” and is supported by
  the repository evidence so an internal reviewer can verify it without
  guessing.

A result scoring 5 is complete and materially better than one omitting a
source, endpoint, interval, or reproducible calculation; a result scoring
below 5 identifies exactly which requirement failed. Also record whether the
worker completed the downstream review/publication handoff. This rubric is
used for every worker comparison from this task's same starting checkpoint;
held-out task judging remains the trial-user/judge responsibility.
